  "notes": [
   "Breadboard hole names follow the image's row letters: the image's upper half is labelled j\u2013f and the lower half e\u2013a. I used bank 'b' for the upper half (rows f\u2013j) and 't' for the lower half (rows a\u2013e), as the catalogue's row letters imply.",
   "Column numbers are read from the picture. The button sits across the centre gap at columns 4 and 6. Only the two lower legs are visible, so the upper legs (4b.f and 6b.f) are assumed. They are in the same columns, across the gap.",
   "Which button ports (1.l, 1.r, 2.l, 2.r) go to which legs is arbitrary, because the button's two sides are symmetric.",
   "Signal path: Arduino pin 2 \u2192 teal wire \u2192 column 6 upper half. Column 6 is the button leg and, through its lower half, the 10k resistor's first leg. The resistor's other leg is in column 9, which a black wire takes to the GND rail.",
   "The orange wire joins button column 4 to the + (5V) rail. A red wire joins Arduino 5V to the same rail. A black wire joins Arduino GND to the \u2212 rail.",
   "Rail hole numbers (bp.1, bp.3, bn.1, bn.7) are estimated from the picture, counting holes from the left end of the bottom rails.",
   "The 10k resistor is stored as value=10000.",
   "The Arduino\u2013USB cable for serial communication is not drawn in the circuit, so it is not included."
  ]
